@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# A view re-seated by a callee outlives the storage it views
+
+**A compiler defect at the pin `7e91730`, found by cycle 0.0.0's probe 08h on 2026-10-15 and
+re-measured by its run.** A use-after-free in safe code. Raised with the cycle's plan by path; the
+workbench registry numbers it, and nothing here does (`../../../../meta/DECISIONS.md`, @@PD-132@@).
+
+## The program, and its control
+
+| file | the reseat | at `7e91730` |
+|---|---|---|
+| [`probe08h_view_reseated.npk`](probe08h_view_reseated.npk) | a callee, `reset(@c, string_bytes(t))`, writes `<-c = Cursor{ src: src, … }` — `c` declared outside the block, `t` inside it | **accepted** (`npkc` exit 0); after the block `c.src[0]` reads the runtime's free poison, 0xAA: **exit 170** at -O0 and through `opt -O2` |
+| `../../refused/probe08g_holder_outlives_view.npk` | the same reseat as a direct assignment in the block | **refused** `NITPICK-BORROW-002` (D-004 rule 3, the compiler's DEF-42) |
+
+Measured at planning with an allocation between the block and the read, the same read answers `z`
+(122) — the freed body handed to the next string.
+
+## Why the compiler lets it through, as read
+
+DEF-42's fix checks a holder declared outside the referent's block at a DIRECT assignment. A store
+done by a callee is judged by the compiler's D-325 summaries — the callee's body stores what it is
+handed — which refuse a view of the CALLER'S FRAME stored into what a parameter points at, measured:
+a view of a callee's local stored into its caller's holder is `NITPICK-BORROW-002` at the call. A
+view of a shorter-lived BLOCK of the same frame is not asked about. A string's body is freed at its
+block's end, so the summary's frame-level answer is too coarse for it.
+
+## What it means here
+
+No function in this library stores a view it was handed into a place it was handed (`SCAN_MODEL.md`
+C-2, @@PD-132@@): a `Cursor` is built once, over the bytes its constructor was given, and only `pos`
+moves. Until the compiler refuses the call, that is a rule the review holds, and the day it does,
+this directory's program moves to `refused/` by a decision that says so.
+
+## How to re-measure
+
+From this directory, with `$NPKC` and `$NPKRT` the pinned toolchain:
+
+```
+$NPKC probe08h_view_reseated.npk -o p.ll ; echo "npkc $?"
+llc -O0 -filetype=obj -relocation-model=static p.ll -o p.o && ld.lld -static p.o "$NPKRT" -o p && ./p ; echo "run $?"
+```
diff --git a/tests/probe/defect/view_reseat/probe08h_view_reseated.npk b/tests/probe/defect/view_reseat/probe08h_view_reseated.npk
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c242d9e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/probe/defect/view_reseat/probe08h_view_reseated.npk
@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+// npkc: 0
+// run: 170 at -O0 and through opt -O2 -- the free poison, 0xAA
+//
+// PROBE 08h -- PROBE 08g's RESEAT, DONE BY A CALLEE. A COMPILER DEFECT AT `7e91730`.
+//
+// ASKS: `reset(@c, string_bytes(t))` where `reset` writes `<-c = Cursor{ src: src, ... }`, `c`
+// declared outside the block and `t` inside it. The direct assignment is refused
+// `NITPICK-BORROW-002` (`refused/probe08g_holder_outlives_view.npk`). Is the call?
+//
+// THE ANSWER AT `7e91730`, measured at planning: ACCEPTED (`npkc` exit 0), and after the block
+// `c.src[0]` reads the runtime's free poison, 0xAA -- exit 170 at -O0 and through `opt -O2`. A
+// use-after-free in safe code. The callee's store is read by the compiler's D-325 summary, which
+// asks whether the view outlives the FRAME; DEF-42's block rule is asked of a direct assignment
+// only. See this directory's `README.md`.
+mod:probe08h_view_reseated;
+
+struct:Cursor = { fixed uint8[]:src; int64:pos; };
+
+func:reset = NIL(Cursor->:c, fixed uint8[]:src) never fails {
+    <-c = Cursor{ src: src, pos: 0i64 };
+    pass NIL;
+};
+
+func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
+    string:s = string_concat("abc", "def");
+    Cursor:c = Cursor{ src: string_bytes(s), pos: 0i64 };
+    if (c.pos == 0i64) {
+        string:t = string_concat("ghij", "klmn");
+        drop reset(@c, string_bytes(t));
+    }
+    if (c.src.len != 8i64) { exit 10i32; }
+    if (c.src[0i64] != 103u8) { exit (c.src[0i64] => int32); }
+    exit 0i32;
+};
+
+func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
+    pick (e) {
+        (HeapBadRequest)    { exit 91i32; },
+        (HeapOom)           { exit 92i32; },
+        (OutOfBounds)       { exit 94i32; },
+        (Unreachable)       { exit 95i32; },
+        (WildLeak)          { exit 96i32; },
+        (StackExhausted)    { exit 106i32; },
+        (MachineFault)      { exit 107i32; },
+        (*)                 { exit 99i32; }
+    }
+    exit 9i32;
+};
